# main.lume — DELIBERATE ERROR PROGRAM (an import cycle).
#
# graph/node.lume imports graph.edge (its line 4).
# graph/edge.lume imports graph.node (its line 4).
#
# Expected: REFUSED, before anything is generated, with an import-cycle error
# that names both files and both import lines and draws the loop — README,
# milestone 9: "Lume modules: ... cycle detection".
#
# What would be a bad message, and what I am really checking for:
#   * a rustc error of any kind (the README promises none ever leak);
#   * "unknown type `edge.Edge`" or "unknown type `node.Node`", which is what
#     a loader that gave up part-way through the loop would say, and which
#     sends the reader looking for a spelling mistake;
#   * an error naming only ONE of the two files, which leaves the reader with
#     half the loop;
#   * a hang or a stack overflow.
#
# The shape matters, because it is not a contrived one. A graph whose Node
# holds Edges and whose Edge holds Nodes is the first data structure a person
# reaches for, and splitting it across two files is the obvious layout. If
# Lume refuses it, the only fix is to put both types in one file — and the
# README never says so. It lists "cycle detection" as a feature and stops.
# The error message is the only documentation this rule has, so it ought to
# end with the fix.
#
# (Were the cycle allowed, this program would print `3` then `0`: `a.degree`
# is 0 because `node` builds every node with an empty edge list and nothing
# here puts `e` back into it.)

import graph.node
import graph.edge

def main:
  a = node.node("a")
  b = node.node("b")
  e = edge.edge(a, b, 3)
  puts e.weight
  puts e.label
  puts a.degree
  puts a.label
